    "title": "Preterite Tense: Regular Verbs",
    "subtitle": "Finished actions in the past: endings, spelling changes and the -ir stem change in the third person",
    "footer": "Spanish Learning Program · A2 · Verbs · Preterite (regular)",
    "next": "Next: A2 · PDF 2 — Preterite: irregular verbs"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "say what you did, in all six persons, with any regular -ar, -er and -ir verb",
        "recognise when to use the preterite and which time words go with it",
        "handle the spelling changes (busqué, llegué, empecé) and the verbs with y (leyó, creyeron)",
        "conjugate the -ir stem-changing verbs in the past (durmió, pidieron)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. What the preterite does"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The preterite (<i>pretérito indefinido</i>) describes an action that is <b>finished</b>: it happened at a specific moment, it happened a certain number of times, or it is one step in a story. Think of it as a camera snapshot of a completed event."
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>A single finished event:</b> <i>Ayer compré un libro.</i> (Yesterday I bought a book.)",
        "<b>A period that is over:</b> <i>Viví en Granada tres años.</i> (I lived in Granada for three years.)",
        "<b>A chain of events:</b> <i>Llegué, abrí la puerta y encendí la luz.</i>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "One tense, two Dutch tenses",
      "bullets": [
        "Dutch uses both <i>ik werkte</i> and <i>ik heb gewerkt</i> for finished events. For a finished event with a past time word, Spanish uses the preterite: <b>Gisteren heb ik gewerkt = Ayer trabajé.</b>",
        "The Spanish <i>he trabajado</i> is not the same as <i>ik heb gewerkt</i>. It is used for a different kind of past (PDF 5)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "spain",
      "title": "Spain vs Latin America",
      "text": "In Spain, <b>hoy</b>, <b>esta mañana</b>, <b>esta semana</b> and <b>este año</b> normally take the present perfect (PDF 5): <i>Hoy he comido tarde.</i> In Latin America the preterite is often used there instead: <i>Hoy comí tarde.</i> With <b>ayer</b>, <b>anoche</b> and other finished periods, both areas use the preterite."
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Time words that signal the preterite"
    },
